   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 3184129,
   "parent_id": 3184118,
   "author_user_id": 57428,
   "author_display_name": "sharptooth",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/3184129",
   "score": 14,
   "created_utc": "2010-07-06T06:59:41Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-07-06T06:59:41Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>If you do so you will run into undefined behavior. Never try that. Although <code>new</code> might be implemented through <code>malloc()</code> and <code>delete</code> might be implemented through <code>free()</code> there's no guarantee that they are really implemented that way <em>and</em> also the user can overload <code>new</code> and <code>delete</code> at his discretion. You risk running  into heap corruption.</p>\n\n<p>Other than that don't forget that when you call <code>malloc()</code> you get raw memory - no constructor is invoked - and when you call <code>free()</code> no destructor is invoked. This can as well lead to undefined behavior and improper functioning of the program.</p>\n\n<p>The bottom line is... never do this. </p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 3184132,
   "parent_id": 3184118,
   "author_user_id": 131140,
   "author_display_name": "Goz",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/3184132",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2010-07-06T07:00:10Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-07-06T07:00:10Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>1) Undefined behaviour but will probably \"work\" though.  Destructors will get called on the memory being freed that pobably doesn't want to be deconstructed.<br>\n2) Undefined behaviour but will probably \"work\" though.  Destructors will NOT get called.</p>\n\n<p>ie IF it works, and there is no guarantee of that, then it will only, likely, work exactly as required for basic builtin data types.</p>\n"
  },
